 120: ## Supabase, authentication, and RLS
 121: 
 122: - API routes and server code use createSupabaseServerClient from
 123:   src/lib/supabase/server-client.ts.
 124: - Client Components use createSupabaseBrowserClient from
 125:   src/lib/supabase/browser-client.ts.
 126: - Server authorization uses auth.getUser(), which validates the JWT.
 127: - Browser session reads may use auth.getSession().
 128: - Never expose SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY to client code.
 129: - Service-role access never replaces application authorization checks.
 130: - Ground SQL in schema and policy evidence. Local drafts may use source and
 131:   migrations with assumptions recorded; verify the actual schema and policies
 132:   on the named target before applying SQL or claiming compatibility. Repository
 133:   documentation may lag behind that target.
 134: - Every migration, RLS policy, repository, or database API change requires the
 135:   Supabase/RLS review gate.
 136: 
 137: ### Application user ID versus Supabase UID
 138: 
 139: The users table has two distinct identifiers:
 140: 
 141: - users.id is the application UUID used by foreign keys.
 142: - users.supabase_uid maps the row to auth.uid().
 143: 
 144: Therefore:
 145: 
 146: - RLS compares users.supabase_uid with auth.uid()::text.
 147: - API routes resolve the application user by supabase_uid.
 148: - Application foreign keys use users.id.
 149: - Comparing users.id directly with auth.uid() is incorrect.
 150: 
 151: Known schema traps:
 152: 
 153: - profiles does not exist; use users.
 154: - messages links to rooms through conversations.room_id, not messages.room_id.
 155: - Roles are admin or member; do not invent new role values.
 156: 
 157: ## Code and architecture rules
 158: 
 159: - Use strict TypeScript. Prefer explicit public boundaries and avoid any.
 160: - Reuse semantic types from src/types before creating new ones.
 161: - Interfaces describe object shapes; type aliases describe unions and function
 162:   signatures.
 163: - Keep business logic in utilities/services, data access in repositories, and
 164:   view concerns in components.
 165: - Query hooks live in src/hooks/queries, mutations in src/hooks/mutations, and
 166:   Realtime hooks in src/hooks/realtime.
 167: - Construct repositories with the server Supabase client in API routes.
 168: - Avoid giant files and condition growth; extract cohesive behavior, not thin
 169:   wrappers.
 170: - Canonical avatar display is EnhancedAvatarV2; upload is UploadableAvatar.
 171: 
 172: ## UI interaction
 173: 
 174: Interactive children inside clickable cards use data-avatar-interactive where
 175: applicable. Parent handlers must ignore interactive descendants such as links,
 176: buttons, role=button, and data-space-action. Portal menu content must stop
 177: pointer, click, and keyboard propagation when needed to prevent accidental room
 178: navigation.
 179: 
 180: ## Test policy
 181: 
 182: Write only integration and E2E tests. Unit tests are forbidden.
 183: 
 184: - Integration tests run against real local infrastructure: the local Supabase
 185:   database, RLS, Storage, and Realtime, or the real API route against it.
 186: - E2E tests drive the real UI with Playwright, using two accounts when the
 187:   behavior involves more than one user.
 188: - A test that mocks Supabase, a repository, or the code under test is a unit
 189:   test and is not allowed, even when it is called integration.
 190: - No new unit tests. When a change touches an existing unit test, replace its
 191:   behavior with integration or E2E coverage and then delete it. Record the
 192:   removal and its replacement in TRACK, or in the handoff outside spec-to-done.
 193: - A spec-to-done SPEC's test strategy uses integration and E2E levels only.
 194: 
 195: ## Verification
 196: 
 197: Choose evidence proportional to risk:
 198: 
 199: - documentation-only changes: check affected instructions, links, metadata,
 200:   and any applicable document/skill validator;
 201: - focused integration or E2E tests for the changed behavior;
 202: - regression tests for the reported failure;
 203: - typecheck and lint for touched TypeScript;
 204: - build when framework boundaries or production output can be affected;
 205: - database/RLS checks against the named target when database behavior is claimed;
 206: - browser smoke tests for user-visible workflows;
 207: - final diff inspection and diff check.
 208: 
 209: Use meaningful regression coverage for changed behavior; do not add tests that
 210: only mirror trivial edits. After checks pass, rerun or broaden them only for
 211: new changes, failures, or unresolved risk. A required runtime check without its
 212: prerequisites remains unverified; it does not block unrelated local checks.
 213: 
 214: Do not weaken assertions or treat skipped critical tests as passing. Mocks do not
 215: prove database concurrency, RLS, Realtime delivery, or multi-user behavior.
 216: 
 217: Common commands:
 218: 
 219: - npm run dev
 220: - npm run type-check
 221: - npm run lint
 222: - npm run build
 223: - npm run test:messaging:db and npm run test:presence:db (DB integration)
 224: - npm run test:messaging:local:e2e and npm run test:presence:e2e (E2E)
 225: - npm test runs the legacy unit suite while it still exists
 226: 
 227: ## Cross-model delegation
 228: 
 229: Use the following policy for cross-provider delegation (OpenAI to Anthropic or
 230: Anthropic to OpenAI). Same-provider specialist agents retain their own contracts.
 231: Pass the full model ID and an explicit effort level; do not rely on runtime defaults or
 232: moving aliases.
 233: 
 234: | Provider | Model ID | Standard work | Important work |
 235: | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 236: | OpenAI | `gpt-6.1-sol` (GPT-6.1 Sol) | `high` | `max` |
 237: | Anthropic | `claude-opus-5-5` (Claude Opus 5.5) | `high` | `xhigh` |
 238: 
 239: Important work includes adversarial reviews, review gates, acceptance checks,
 240: security-critical changes, and difficult investigations where the first fix
 241: failed or the root cause remains uncertain. Use the important-work effort from
