   1: # SPEC: Phase 5 — Messaging Resilience
   2: 
   3: Goal: In the production messaging drawer, no message a user sends is lost or duplicated across network and 
Realtime outages, reloads, several tabs, and several devices; typing, unread state, and @mention notifications stay 
live and correct; and messaging health is observable through redacted logs — without regressing existing messaging.
   4: 
   5: Status: Draft — pending adversarial review and owner approval. Interview rounds 1–2 and confirmation recorded in 
`state.md` (Verdict: Ready).
   6: 
   7: ## 1. Summary
   8: 
   9: The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, 
state in `MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` 
idempotency), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group messages. This phase 
adds: a persistent per-user offline outbox for text messages; a connection banner; automatic reconnection of every 
messaging Realtime channel with polling fallback; a reliable, named, members-only typing indicator; live cross-device 
unread sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server logs; 
and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open conversation after reload.
  10: 
  11: ## 2. Problem statement
  12: 
  13: Workspace members using the chat on unstable networks or sleeping laptops lose work and trust: a message sent 
offline fails and must be retried by hand, a reload discards it, and nothing tells the user the chat is disconnected. 
If Realtime drops, the conversation-list channel never reconnects by itself and there is no fallback, so new messages 
and unread counts silently stop updating. The typing indicator disappears after ~4 s of continuous typing and is 
broadcast on a public channel. Reading a conversation on one device leaves stale unread badges on the user's other 
devices. There is no way to mention someone, so room conversations (which never notify) cannot reach a specific 
person. Nobody can tell in production whether messaging is healthy.
  14: 
  15: ## 3. Goals and success metrics
  16: 
  17: ### Goals
  18: - G1: A text message the user sends is never silently lost: it is delivered, pending, or visibly failed with an 
action.
  19: - G2: No message is ever stored or shown twice because of automatic resends, several tabs, or lost responses.
  20: - G3: Messaging recovers from network and Realtime outages without user action, and keeps updating through 
polling while Realtime is down.
  21: - G4: Typing indicators are reliable, named, and visible only to conversation members.
  22: - G5: Unread/read state and own sent messages stay in sync live across the same user's tabs and devices.
  23: - G6: A user can @mention conversation members, and the mentioned person is notified on the desktop, including 
in rooms.
  24: - G7: Messaging health (latency, delivery, connection) is observable in technical logs without exposing content.
  25: - G8: No existing messaging behavior regresses; every database change reaches the online target in a controlled, 
verified way at phase end.
  26: 
  27: ### Success metrics
  28: - Pending messages are delivered within 10 s after connectivity returns, without any click, in their original 
order, each exactly once.
  29: - With Realtime down and HTTP working, other members' new messages appear within 45 s of the outage start, and 
within 15 s of being sent once polling is active (plus request time).
  30: - Each flow above is covered by a passing two-account (and, where relevant, two-tab / two-device) Playwright E2E 
test that passed 3 consecutive runs.
  31: - The existing messaging regression E2E suite passes after each slice.
  32: - Every migration written in this phase is applied to the online target `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` at phase end with 
explicit owner authorization, catalog readback, and a runtime smoke check.
  33: - Owner final test (new app against the online database) accepts the phase.
  34: 
  35: ## 4. Users and stakeholders
  36: 
  37: ### Primary users
  38: - All workspace members who use the chat in direct, group, and room conversations (members of 
`conversation_members`), as sender, recipient, or mentioned user.
  39: 
  40: ### Secondary users
  41: - The same user on several tabs of one browser and on several devices/browsers at once.
  42: - The owner/operator, who reads the messaging metric logs.
  43: 
  44: ### Stakeholders
  45: - Owner (Giuliano): approves the SPEC, performs the final test, authorizes the online migrations at phase end, 
and deploys the application.
  46: 
  47: ## 5. Scope
  48: 
  49: ### In scope
  50: - Persistent offline outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) for text messages and replies; 
pending state in the feed; cancel; ordered automatic drain; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h expiry handling; 
session-expiry and sign-out rules.
  51: - Connection banner in the drawer ("Sem conexão" / "Reconectando…").
  52: - Automatic reconnection with exponential backoff for every messaging Realtime channel (feed/receipts/reactions 
channel and the conversation-list channel) with catch-up.
  53: - Polling fallback after > 30 s of Realtime outage, while the tab is visible.
  54: - Typing indicator: continuous display while typing, names, DM/group/room, private members-only channel, own 
sessions excluded, expiry.
  55: - Live cross-device/tab sync of unread counters and read state, and of the user's own sent messages.
  56: - @mentions: autocomplete of conversation members, highlighted mention, server-validated mention records, 
desktop notification (DM, group, room, thread reply), feed highlight for the mentioned user.
  57: - Messaging metrics as redacted structured server logs via an authenticated application route.
  58: - Phase 4 follow-ups: membership check inside `mark_conversation_read`; `get_unread_counts` not executable by 
`anon`; restore the open conversation after page reload.
  59: 
  60: ### Out of scope
  61: - Queuing attachments or voice notes offline (they keep the Phase 4 composer failure + "Tentar de novo").
  62: - Editing a pending message (cancel and rewrite instead).
  63: - Web Push / service worker / notifications with the browser or tab closed.
  64: - `@todos` / `@here`; a separate mention-only notification preference; scrolling to the message on notification 
click.
  65: - Draft persistence (same device or across devices); live sync of stars; cross-device dismissal of notifications.
  66: - Usage/engagement metrics (messages per day, active conversations); a metrics UI or external analytics service.
  67: - Safari and mobile browsers; drawer layout below ~400 px.
  68: - Other Phase 4 follow-ups: pending-upload cap, toaster covering the composer, pin no-op/English copy, orphan 
storage objects, "Favoritas" discoverability.
  69: - Deploying the application (owner action).
  70: 
  71: ## 6. User journeys
  72: 
  73: - J1 Offline send: A loses internet, keeps writing; each message appears as "pendente" with a clock; the banner 
says "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar". Internet returns; within 10 s every pending message is 
delivered in order and B sees each once.
  74: - J2 Reload while offline: A reloads with pending messages; they are still pending after reload and deliver when 
back online.
  75: - J3 Two tabs: A has two tabs; the pending message shows in both; only one tab sends it; cancelling in either 
removes it from both.
  76: - J4 Rejection: A's pending message targets a room A has since lost access to; it turns into a visible failure 
with the text kept and "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; later pending messages for that room wait; messages for other 
conversations still deliver.
  77: - J5 Sign-out: A clicks Sair with 2 pending messages; a dialog warns that leaving deletes them; A chooses 
Cancelar or Sair mesmo.
  78: - J6 Realtime outage: the WebSocket drops; after 5 s the banner shows "Reconectando…"; after 30 s the open 
conversation and the list update every 15 s by polling; when Realtime returns, polling stops and the banner disappears.
  79: - J7 Typing: B types continuously in a group; A sees "Bruno está digitando…" the whole time; it disappears ≤ 5 s 
after B stops.
  80: - J8 Two devices: A reads conversation X on the laptop; the desktop's badge for X clears without reload.
  81: - J9 Mention: A types "@" in a room chat, picks Bruno; Bruno's tab is hidden; Bruno gets "Ana mencionou você em 
<sala>" with the full message text; clicking opens the room; the message is highlighted for Bruno.
  82: - J10 Reload restore: A reloads with the drawer open on conversation X; the drawer reopens on X.
  83: 
  84: ## 7. Functional requirements
  85: 
  86: | ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance signal |
  87: |---|---|---|---|
  88: | FR-001 | When A sends a message with text and/or a reply target and no attachments, and the browser is offline 
or the create request fails transiently (network error, timeout, 5xx, rate limit), the message is stored in A's 
persistent outbox and shown in the feed as pending (clock icon + "pendente" text) instead of the Phase 4 composer 
failure; the composer clears. | Must | AC-001, AC-003 |
  89: | FR-002 | Pending messages are sent automatically when connectivity returns (browser online, successful 
Realtime reconnection, or periodic retry), per conversation in creation order, one in flight per conversation, reusing 
each message's `clientMessageId`; delivered within 10 s after connectivity returns; the pending item becomes the 
normal sent message. | Must | AC-002, AC-038 |
  90: | FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and closing the tab; pending messages of the current user appear in every 
tab of the same browser; at most one tab sends a given pending message at a time. | Must | AC-004, AC-005 |
  91: | FR-004 | A pending message that is not currently in flight can be cancelled from any tab; it disappears from 
all tabs and is never sent. While in flight it shows "enviando" without cancel. | Must | AC-006 |
  92: | FR-005 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic attempts for that message; it shows as failed in the 
feed with its text preserved and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later pending messages of the same 
conversation wait until it is resolved; other conversations are unaffected. | Must | AC-007, AC-008 |
  93: | FR-006 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the message stays pending and is retried automatically (honoring 
a server retry delay when provided), preserving order and without duplicates. | Must | AC-009 |
  94: | FR-007 | A pending message older than 24 h from when the user pressed send is never sent automatically; it 
shows as failed ("expirada") with "Tentar de novo" (manual send) and "Apagar". | Must | AC-010 |
  95: | FR-008 | A message with attachments or a voice note is not queued: offline or on failure it keeps the Phase 4 
behavior (stays in the composer with failure message and "Tentar de novo"). | Must | AC-011 |
  96: | FR-009 | The outbox is scoped to the application user (`users.id`) on that browser. If the session ends 
without an explicit sign-out (expiry, revocation), the outbox is kept and drains only after the same user signs in 
again (subject to FR-007); another user signing in on that browser never sees, sends, or deletes it. | Must | AC-012 |
  97: | FR-010 | When the user clicks Sair (any in-app sign-out entry point) with N > 0 pending/failed outbox 
messages, a dialog shows "Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" (deletes 
that user's outbox, then signs out) and "Cancelar" (keeps session and outbox). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | 
Must | AC-013 |
  98: | FR-011 | The drawer shows a discreet banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" as soon as 
the browser reports offline; "Reconectando…" when messaging Realtime has been disconnected for more than 5 s while the 
browser is online; the banner disappears by itself once connectivity and Realtime are restored. | Must | AC-014 |
  99: | FR-012 | Every messaging Realtime channel (messages/receipts/reactions and the conversation list) reconnects 
automatically after `TIMED_OUT`, `CHANNEL_ERROR`, or `CLOSED`, with exponential backoff and a capped delay, resumes 
subscriptions without user action, and runs a catch-up refetch of the open feed and the conversation list on resume. | 
Must | AC-015 |
 100: | FR-013 | Polling fallback: while messaging Realtime has been disconnected for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages and the conversation list (unread counts) every 15 s; 
when the tab becomes visible while still disconnected it fetches immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while the 
tab is hidden; polling stops once Realtime is connected again. | Must | AC-016 |
 101: | FR-014 | The typing indicator stays visible while the other person types continuously and disappears at most 5 
s after they stop typing, blur the composer, send, leave the conversation, or close the tab. | Must | AC-017 |
 102: | FR-015 | The indicator names typists: 1 → "Ana está digitando…", 2 → "Ana e Bruno estão digitando…", 3+ → "N 
pessoas estão digitando…", in direct, group, and room conversations. | Must | AC-018 |
 103: | FR-016 | Typing signals travel on a private Realtime channel authorized so that only members of that 
conversation can join and receive them. | Must | AC-019 |
 104: | FR-017 | A user never sees their own typing indicator, including from their other tabs or devices. | Must | 
AC-020 |
 105: | FR-018 | When the user reads messages on one tab/device, the unread counters and read state on their other 
tabs/devices update live, without reload, focus change, or reconnect. | Must | AC-021 |
 106: | FR-019 | A message the user sends from one device/tab appears on their other devices/tabs live. | Must | 
AC-022 |
 107: | FR-020 | In the composer, typing "@" opens a list of the conversation's current members (excluding the user), 
filtered as they type, operable by mouse and keyboard (arrows, Enter/Tab, Esc); choosing inserts a highlighted 
mention. In a room the list contains only members of the room conversation. | Must | AC-023 |
 108: | FR-021 | The server records a message's mentions as application user IDs and accepts only users who are 
members of the conversation at send time (excluding the sender); any other "@name" stays plain text and creates no 
mention. | Must | AC-024 |
 109: | FR-022 | With the Phase 4 desktop-notification toggle on and permission granted, a mentioned user receives a 
