 139: ## 9. Data and integrations
 140: 
 141: ### Data inputs
 142: - Composer text, reply target, and mention selections (user IDs) per message.
 143: - Browser connectivity events (`online`/`offline`), Realtime channel status, tab visibility.
 144: - Server responses from `POST /api/messages/create` (status, `clientMessageId` replay, rate-limit retry delay 
when present).
 145: 
 146: ### Data outputs
 147: - Browser IndexedDB outbox, keyed by application user ID: `clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply 
target, mention user IDs, created-at, attempt state, failure reason. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared across 
users.
 148: - Database: message mention records (message ID + mentioned application user ID), readable under the same 
conversation-membership rules as the message.
 149: - Realtime authorization policy for private typing topics (members only).
 150: - `mark_conversation_read` with an internal membership check; `get_unread_counts` without `anon` EXECUTE.
 151: - Structured metric log lines (BR-013) from an authenticated application route.
 152: - Persisted drawer state per user: open flag + active conversation ID (browser storage).
 153: 
 154: ### Integrations
 155: - Supabase Postgres, Realtime (`postgres_changes`, private broadcast topics with RLS on `realtime.messages`), 
Auth (server routes use `createSupabaseServerClient` + `auth.getUser()`).
 156: - Browser APIs: IndexedDB, Web Locks/BroadcastChannel or equivalent for cross-tab coordination, Notification API 
(existing Phase 4), Page Visibility.
 157: - Hosting logs (Vercel) and/or Supabase logs as the metrics sink — no new external analytics dependency.
 158: 
 159: ## 10. Constraints and assumptions
 160: 
 161: ### Constraints
 162: - Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase, TanStack Query; reuse existing hooks, repositories, API 
routes, and query keys (`['messages', id]`, `['conversations', userId]`, …) rather than parallel implementations.
 163: - Identity: RLS compares `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text`; foreign keys use `users.id`; messages 
link to rooms through `conversations.room_id`.
 164: - Browsers: Chrome and Edge desktop (automated), Firefox desktop. Safari and mobile not required.
 165: - Background notifications are page-level only (tab open but hidden); no service worker.
 166: - No message text or names in metrics (BR-013); no new external analytics service.
 167: - The sign-out confirmation must not change the existing presence logout sequence (presence-safety rules apply 
to that touch point).
 168: - Online database changes only at phase end with explicit authorization (BR-014); the hosted app currently runs 
the pre-Phase-4 build until the owner deploys.
 169: - Repository test policy (BR-015); type-check, lint, and build must pass.
 170: 
 171: ### Assumptions
 172: - None beyond the user-confirmed answers recorded in `state.md`.
 173: 
 174: ## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
 175: 
 176: | Case | Expected behavior |
 177: |---|---|
 178: | Request reaches the server but the response is lost | Message stays pending; the retry reuses 
`clientMessageId`; server returns the stored message; exactly one message exists (AC-038). |
 179: | Two tabs come online at the same time with the same outbox | Only one tab sends each message; each is stored 
and shown once (AC-005). |
 180: | User cancels in tab 2 while tab 1 is about to send | Cancel only while waiting; once in flight the item shows 
"enviando" without cancel; no message sent after a completed cancel (AC-006). |
 181: | Lost access to the conversation before reconnect | First message fails definitively with text kept; later 
messages of that conversation wait; other conversations deliver (AC-007). |
 182: | Access regained after a definitive failure | "Tentar de novo" delivers it; then the waiting messages deliver 
in order (AC-008). |
 183: | Server rate limit during drain | Messages stay pending and retry automatically; order and uniqueness preserved 
(AC-009). |
 184: | Laptop asleep > 24 h with pending messages | They become "expirada" failures, not sent automatically (AC-010). 
|
 185: | Offline message with attachment or voice note | Not queued; Phase 4 composer failure + retry (AC-011). |
 186: | Session expires while messages are pending; another user signs in on the same browser | The other user never 
sees or sends them; the original user's messages drain after they sign in again (AC-012). |
 187: | User clicks Sair with pending messages | Confirmation dialog; Cancelar keeps everything; Sair mesmo deletes 
the outbox and signs out (AC-013). |
 188: | IndexedDB unavailable (private mode, blocked storage) | Queue cannot persist: message keeps the Phase 4 
composer failure + "Tentar de novo" — never silently dropped (AC-039). |
 189: | Realtime drop shorter than 5 s | No "Reconectando…" banner (AC-014). |
 190: | Realtime down > 30 s, tab hidden | No polling; on becoming visible, fetch immediately then every 15 s 
(AC-016). |
 191: | Realtime back after polling | Polling requests stop; catch-up shows no duplicates (AC-015, AC-016). |
 192: | Typist closes the tab or loses connection mid-typing | Indicator disappears ≤ 5 s (AC-017). |
 193: | Non-member tries to join a typing topic | Join refused / no events received (AC-019). |
 194: | "@name" for a non-member or forged mention ID in a direct API call | No mention recorded, no notification 
(AC-024). |
 195: | Mention in a DM that would also notify as a DM | Exactly one notification (AC-026). |
 196: | Mentioned user is viewing that conversation in a visible tab | No notification (AC-026). |
 197: | Metrics route down or slow | Messaging unaffected, no visible error (AC-029). |
 198: | Restored conversation no longer accessible after reload | Conversation list opens without error (AC-032). |
 199: 
 200: ## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
 201: - Outbox content lives only in the user's browser, scoped to their application user ID, and is deleted on 
explicit sign-out after confirmation; it is never exposed to another user on a shared browser.
 202: - Typing topics become private and members-only (Realtime RLS on `realtime.messages`, using `users.supabase_uid 
= auth.uid()::text` → `users.id` membership).
 203: - Mentions are validated server-side against conversation membership; clients cannot notify or tag non-members 
by forging IDs; mention records are readable only by conversation members.
 204: - Notifications never leak content of conversations the user is not a member of.
 205: - Metric logs follow BR-013 (no content, names, or identifiers); the metrics route requires authentication and 
bounds payload size and batch length to avoid log abuse.
 206: - `mark_conversation_read` membership check and `anon` revoke on `get_unread_counts` close two Phase 4 findings.
 207: - No service-role key in client code; service-role use never replaces application authorization checks.
 208: - Every migration, RLS policy, route, and repository change passes the Supabase/RLS review gate.
 209: 
 210: ## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations
 211: - UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese, using the exact strings in this SPEC where given.
 212: - Connection banner uses `role="status"` / polite live region; pending, sending, failed, and expired states have 
text labels, not only icons.
 213: - Pending-item actions ("Cancelar", "Tentar de novo", "Apagar") and the sign-out dialog are keyboard-operable 
with visible focus; the dialog traps focus and Esc means Cancelar.
 214: - The mention list follows the combobox/listbox pattern (arrow keys, Enter/Tab to select, Esc to close) and is 
announced to screen readers.
 215: - The mention highlight does not rely on color alone.
 216: 
 217: ## 14. Acceptance criteria
 218: 
 219: - AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is offline, when A sends a text message in a conversation 
with B, then A's feed shows it as pending with a clock and the text "pendente", the composer is empty, and B does not 
see it.
 220: - AC-002 (FR-002, BR-003, BR-004): Given A queued 3 messages offline in conversation X, when connectivity 
returns, then within 10 s and without any click B sees exactly those 3 messages, once each, in the original order, and 
A's items become normal sent messages.
 221: - AC-003 (FR-001, BR-005): Given the browser reports online but A's create request fails with a network error, 
when A sends, then the message becomes pending (not a composer failure) and is delivered automatically once requests 
succeed.
 222: - AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending message, when A reloads the page while still offline, then the message 
is still pending; when back online it is delivered exactly once.
 223: - AC-005 (FR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A has two tabs open, when A queues 2 messages offline in tab 
1, then both appear as pending in tab 2; when both tabs come back online simultaneously, then B receives each message 
exactly once and the database holds exactly one row per `clientMessageId`.
 224: - AC-006 (FR-004, BR-007): Given a waiting pending message, when A cancels it in tab 2, then it disappears from 
both tabs and is never delivered after reconnect; given an in-flight item, then it shows "enviando" and no cancel 
action.
 225: - AC-007 (FR-005, BR-003, BR-005): Given A queued m1 and m2 in room conversation X and m3 in DM Y, and A lost 
access to X before reconnecting, when connectivity returns, then m1 shows as failed with its text and "Tentar de novo" 
/ "Apagar", m2 stays waiting and is not sent, and m3 is delivered.
 226: - AC-008 (FR-005, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, 
then m1 and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 
is attempted next.
 227: - AC-009 (FR-006, BR-005): Given A's drain hits the server's create rate limit, then the rejected messages stay 
pending and are retried automatically, and B finally sees all of them in order, once each.
 228: - AC-010 (FR-007, BR-006): Given a pending message whose send time is more than 24 h ago, when connectivity 
returns, then it is not sent and shows as failed "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; "Tentar de novo" 
delivers it once.
 229: - AC-011 (FR-008, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A sends a message with a file or a voice note, then it stays 
in the composer with the Phase 4 failure message and "Tentar de novo", and nothing is added to the outbox.
 230: - AC-012 (FR-009, BR-001; must-not R1-q16 A): Given A has pending messages and A's session ends without sign-out 
(session revoked server-side), when C signs in on the same browser, then C sees none of A's pending messages and none 
is sent as C; when A later signs in again, A's messages (< 24 h) are delivered as A.
 231: - AC-013 (FR-010, BR-008): Given A has 2 outbox messages, when A clicks Sair, then the dialog shows "Você tem 2 
mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" / "Cancelar"; Cancelar keeps the session and 
both messages; Sair mesmo signs out, and after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 outbox messages, Sair 
signs out without a dialog and the presence logout behaves as before.
 232: - AC-014 (FR-011): Given the drawer is open, when the browser goes offline, then "Sem conexão — mensagens serão 
enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when Realtime stays 
down for more than 5 s, "Reconectando…" appears; when connectivity and Realtime return, the banner disappears without 
user action.
 233: - AC-015 (FR-012): Given both the messages channel and the conversation-list channel lose Realtime, when 
Realtime becomes available again, then both resubscribe without user action, B's messages sent during the outage 
appear once, unread counts are correct, and the recorded reconnect events show increasing, capped retry delays.
 234: - AC-016 (FR-013, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, 
when B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 45 s; a message B sends after polling started 
appears within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts also update. When A's tab is hidden, no polling 
requests are made; when it becomes visible, a fetch happens immediately. After Realtime reconnects, no polling request 
occurs during the next 60 s.
 235: - AC-017 (FR-014; must-not R1-q16 E): Given B types continuously for 15 s, then A sees the typing indicator at 
every 1 s sample; when B stops, it disappears within 5 s; when B closes the tab mid-typing, it disappears within 5 s.
 236: - AC-018 (FR-015): Given a group or room with A, B, C, D, when B types, A sees "<B> está digitando…"; when B and 
C type, "<B> e <C> estão digitando…"; when B, C, and D type, "3 pessoas estão digitando…"; the same applies in a 
direct conversation for one typist.
 237: - AC-019 (FR-016, BR-009): Given authenticated non-member E, when E tries to join conversation X's typing topic 
with the Realtime client, then the join is refused or E receives no typing events, while member A receives B's typing 
events (integration test against local Realtime).
 238: - AC-020 (FR-017, BR-009): Given A has two tabs and a second device on conversation X, when A types in tab 1, 
then neither tab 2 nor the second device shows A as typing.
 239: - AC-021 (FR-018): Given A is signed in on two devices (separate browser contexts) with 3 unread messages in X, 
when A reads them on device 1, then device 2's unread counter for X goes to 0 without reload, focus change, or 
reconnect.
 240: - AC-022 (FR-019): Given A is signed in on two devices, when A sends a message on device 1, then it appears on 
device 2 without reload.
 241: - AC-023 (FR-020, BR-010): Given A types "@" in a room conversation, then the list shows only room-conversation 
members other than A, filters as A types, and supports arrows/Enter/Tab/Esc and mouse; choosing "Bruno" inserts a 
highlighted mention; a workspace user who never joined the room chat is not listed.
 242: - AC-024 (FR-021, BR-010; must-not R1-q16 F): Given a direct API request that sends a message mentioning a 
non-member user ID and the sender's own ID, then the message is stored with no mention record for either and no 
notification reaches the non-member (integration test on the real API route and local DB).
 243: - AC-025 (FR-022, BR-011): Given B has notifications enabled and permission granted, B's tab hidden, when A 
mentions B in a room, then B receives one notification titled "<A> mencionou você em <sala>" with the full text; 
clicking it opens that room conversation in the drawer; when A sends a room message without mentioning B, B receives 
none.
 244: - AC-026 (FR-022, BR-011; must-not R1-q16 C): Given B has notifications enabled, then a mention in a DM yields 
exactly one notification; a mention in a thread reply yields a notification; a mention while B views that conversation 
in a visible tab yields none; A's own message mentioning A yields none for A; with the toggle off, none.
 245: - AC-027 (FR-023): Given A's message mentions B, then all members see the mention styled distinctly, and only B 
sees the whole message highlighted (with a non-color cue).
 246: - AC-028 (FR-024, BR-013): Given a send, a reconnect, and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route 
receives the corresponding events and writes structured log lines with event type, reason, and durations; a scan of 
those lines finds no message text, conversation or user names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; an unauthenticated 
request to the route is rejected and writes nothing.
 247: - AC-029 (FR-025): Given the metrics route returns errors, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then all 
messaging behaves as in AC-001/AC-002 and no error is shown to A.
 248: - AC-030 (FR-026): Given user E is not a member of conversation X, when `mark_conversation_read` is invoked for 
E and X through every role that can execute it, then X's read state for E is not changed and the call is refused 
(integration test on local DB).
 249: - AC-031 (FR-027): Given the `anon` role, when it calls `get_unread_counts`, then permission is denied; an 
authenticated member still gets correct counts (integration test on local DB).
 250: - AC-032 (FR-028, BR-016): Given A has the drawer open on X, when A reloads, then the drawer reopens on X; given 
A lost access to X, then the conversation list opens with no error; given C signs in on that browser, then C does not 
see A's conversation restored; given the drawer was closed, it stays closed.
 251: - AC-033 (FR-029): The existing Phase 4 messaging E2E regression suite and DB integration suite pass after each 
slice; `npm run type-check`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run build` pass.
 252: - AC-034 (BR-014): Every migration of the phase is applied locally with recorded rollback SQL; at phase end, 
after explicit owner authorization, it is applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko`, the catalog is read back from that 
target, and a runtime smoke check passes; the hand-off states the app is ready to deploy after the migrations.
 253: - AC-035 (§12): The Supabase/RLS review gate passes for every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and 
API route change.
 254: - AC-036 (BR-015): Each new E2E spec passed 3 consecutive runs; no unit test was added; every touched unit test 
was replaced by integration/E2E coverage and deleted, recorded in TRACK.
 255: - AC-037 (FR-011, §13): The banner is announced via a polite live region, pending/failed/expired states have 
text labels, and the sign-out dialog and mention list are fully keyboard-operable.
 256: - AC-038 (FR-002, BR-004): Given A's create request reaches the server but the response is dropped, when the 
message is retried automatically, then exactly one message exists and B sees it once.
 257: - AC-039 (FR-001, R1-q2 A): Given IndexedDB is unavailable, when A sends a text message while offline, then it 
is not lost: it stays in the composer with the Phase 4 failure message and "Tentar de novo".
