 118: ## 8. Business rules
 119: 
 120: | ID | Rule | Rationale |
 121: |---|---|---|
 122: | BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user (`users.id`) on one browser. It is never read, sent, or 
deleted while another user is signed in. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A |
 123: | BR-002 | Only text messages and replies without attachments/voice are queued. | R1-q4 A |
 124: | BR-003 | Per conversation, outbox messages are sent in creation order, one in flight at a time; a failed 
(definitive or expired) message blocks later messages of that conversation until the user retries or deletes it. 
Conversations are independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E |
 125: | BR-004 | Each outbox message gets a stable `clientMessageId` when the user presses send; every attempt 
(automatic, manual, any tab) reuses it; the server stores at most one message per (sender, conversation, 
`clientMessageId`). | R1-q2 B, R1-q16 B |
 126: | BR-005 | Send outcome classes: transient = browser offline, network error, timeout, HTTP 5xx, rate limit (429) 
→ stay pending and retry automatically; session = 401 → hold until the same user is authenticated again; definitive = 
no access / conversation not found (403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT`, 404), invalid content (400, 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 422), 
`CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` (409) → failed, no automatic retry. | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A |
 127: | BR-006 | Expiry: a message still unsent 24 h after the user pressed send becomes failed ("expirada") and is 
never sent automatically; a manual "Tentar de novo" sends it with the same `clientMessageId`. | R1-q5 D, R2-q3 B |
 128: | BR-007 | Cancel is available only while a message waits (not in flight). A cancelled message is removed from 
the outbox in all tabs and never sent. | R1-q5 B, R2-q7 A |
 129: | BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with outbox messages requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's 
outbox. A sign-out not initiated by the user (expiry, revocation) keeps it. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
 130: | BR-009 | Typing: only conversation members receive typing signals; a user's own typing (any session) is never 
shown to them; an indicator expires ≤ 5 s after the last typing signal. | R1-q8 A, D, E; R1-q16 E |
 131: | BR-010 | Mentions: only users who are current members of the conversation at send time can be mentioned 
(rooms: members of the room conversation); self-mentions are not recorded; `@todos`/`@here` do not exist. | R1-q10 A, 
E; R2-q10 A |
 132: | BR-011 | Desktop-notification eligibility (extends Phase 4): toggle on and permission granted; never for the 
user's own message; never while that conversation is in view in a visible tab; DM/group: every new message; room: only 
messages that mention the user; at most one notification per message across the user's tabs. | R1-q10 B, R1-q16 C, 
R2-q8 A, R2-q9 A–C |
 133: | BR-012 | Polling runs only while messaging Realtime is disconnected for more than 30 s and the tab is visible, 
every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and stops when Realtime reconnects. | R1-q7 A, R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
 134: | BR-013 | Metric log lines contain only event type, outcome/reason, durations/counts, conversation type, 
timestamp, and app version — never message text, attachment names, conversation names, user names or emails, or 
user/conversation identifiers. | R1-q12 B, R2-q2, confirmation point 9 |
 135: | BR-014 | Database rollout: migrations stay local during the phase (written + applied locally with recorded 
rollback SQL); at phase end they are applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` only with explicit owner authorization, 
followed by catalog readback and a runtime smoke check; the application is deployed by the owner after the migrations; 
rollback is app first, then migrations. | R1-q13 A |
 136: | BR-015 | Tests: only integration (real local Supabase/API route) and E2E (real UI, Playwright); no new unit 
tests; a touched unit test is replaced by integration/E2E coverage and deleted, recorded in TRACK. Each new E2E spec 
counts only after 3 consecutive passing runs. | AGENTS.md test policy, R2-q13 A |
 137: | BR-016 | Conversation restore is per browser and per user: a different user signing in never reopens the 
previous user's conversation; a closed drawer stays closed. | R2-q11 A, B, D |
